  "id": "Compound::DB00788 - Gene::9356",
  "verbalization": "Naproxen is implicated in the regulation of Slc22a6 through multiple interconnected pathways, with the core mechanism involving its interaction with the monocarboxylic acid pathway. The evidence suggests that Naproxen, through its inclusion in the class of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory compounds, binds to Slc22a6, which is a transporter involved in the uptake of monocarboxylic acids. This binding is supported by the lowest common ancestor relationship between Naproxen and Sulindac, both of which are part of the same pathway, and the direct interaction between Sulindac and Slc22a6. Additionally, Naproxen's role in causing Epidermolysis Bullosa and its association with Furosemide, which binds to Slc22a6, further reinforces the involvement of Slc22a6 in the transport of monocarboxylic acids. The presence of Etodolac, another nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, which also binds to Slc22a6, highlights the broader context of Naproxen's influence on Slc22a6 through the monocarboxylic acid pathway. These findings collectively suggest that Naproxen exerts a modulatory effect on Slc22a6, likely through its role in the transport of monocarboxylic acids, and that the observed interactions are biologically plausible and mechanistically consistent.",
